## SvelteKit app

The app lives in `web/`: SvelteKit with Svelte 5, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS 4 and shadcn-svelte, built as a static single-page app. The Go binary embeds the build and serves it next to the API, so production is one binary and one origin, with no Node.js at runtime.

You need Go, [Task](https://taskfile.dev), Node.js 22.17 or newer (24 LTS recommended) and pnpm 12.9.1 (`corepack enable` or `npm install --global pnpm@12.9.1`).

```bash
task dev    # Go server and Vite dev server with hot reload; open the Vite URL
task check  # svelte-check, zero errors and warnings
task build  # pnpm build, then bin/server with the app embedded
```

Without a build, `go build` still works and pages answer "The web app is not built. Run task build or task dev." The Dockerfile builds the app in a Node.js stage and copies only the binary into the runtime image.
{{- end }}
{{- if .MCP }}

## MCP server

Your users connect their AI agents to `/mcp` on this server, for example `https://example.com/mcp`. It speaks MCP over Streamable HTTP and accepts only personal API tokens as `Authorization: Bearer pat_...`. A `read` token can call the read tools; changes need a token with `read` and `write`.

Tools: `whoami`{{ if .Workspaces }}, `list_workspaces`, `get_workspace`, `list_activity` and `get_billing` (read), `invite_member` and `rename_workspace` (write). A token is bound to one workspace, and the workspace tools stay inside it{{ else }} and `get_billing`{{ end }}. Checkout, billing management, deletions, role changes, member removal, admin, passwords and email are never tools.

Claude Code:

```bash
claude mcp add --transport http app https://example.com/mcp --header "Authorization: Bearer pat_..."
```

Claude Desktop and claude.ai: open Customize, Connectors, Add custom connector. Enter the `/mcp` URL, choose "No sign in" under Authentication, and add the request header `Authorization` with the value `Bearer pat_...`. Claude connects from Anthropic's cloud, so the URL must be reachable from the internet. Request headers on custom connectors are in beta and not available to every organization yet. For a server on your machine, or when your organization has no Request headers field, add the local `mcp-remote` bridge to `claude_desktop_config.json` instead:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "app": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["mcp-remote", "http://localhost:8090/mcp", "--header", "Authorization:${AUTH_HEADER}"],
      "env": {"AUTH_HEADER": "Bearer pat_..."}
    }
  }
}
```

Cursor, in `.cursor/mcp.json` with the token in the `API_TOKEN` environment variable:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "app": {
      "url": "https://example.com/mcp",
      "headers": {"Authorization": "Bearer ${env:API_TOKEN}"}
    }
  }
}
```

VS Code, in `.vscode/mcp.json`. VS Code asks for the token once and stores it:

```json
{
  "inputs": [
    {"type": "promptString", "id": "api-token", "description": "Personal API token", "password": true}
  ],
  "servers": {
    "app": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "https://example.com/mcp",
      "headers": {"Authorization": "Bearer ${input:api-token}"}
    }
  }
}
```

Add your own tool next to its service. The services a tool may call are listed in `mcp.Services` in `internal/mcp/server.go` and passed in `internal/routes/routes.go`. This example is tested in `internal/mcp/example_test.go`; inside `internal/mcp`, drop the `mcp.` prefixes and call it from `registerTools` in `internal/mcp/tools.go`:

```go
type nameInput struct {
	Name string `json:"name" jsonschema:"the new display name"`
}

func registerNameTool(server *mcpsdk.Server, services mcp.Services) {
	mcpsdk.AddTool(server, &mcpsdk.Tool{
		Name:        "set_display_name",
		Description: "Changes the display name of the user this token acts for. Needs a token with read and write access.",
	}, mcp.Write(func(ctx context.Context, caller mcp.Caller, input nameInput) (api.UserResponse, error) {
		err := validation.ValidateName(input.Name)
		if err != nil {
			return api.UserResponse{}, mcp.InvalidField("name", api.FieldMessage(err))
		}
		user, err := services.Users.UpdateName(caller.User.ID, input.Name)
		if err != nil {
			return api.UserResponse{}, err
		}
		return api.UserResponse{User: api.NewUser(user)}, nil
	}))
}
```

`mcp.Write` checks the `write` scope before the body runs, and errors come back with the JSON API codes.
